No. I am saying those facts do not establish that the wording is viewpoint neutral.

The attorneys may have:

- reviewed different wording or accompanying definitions;
- relied on precedent from another federal circuit;
- assessed litigation risk rather than concluded the rule was constitutional;
- expected Rule 2 to be applied only to objectively disruptive conduct; or
- reached a legal judgment with which a court could disagree.

It is also unknown whether the League formally endorsed this exact text, whether any peer city has enforced it, or whether a court has reviewed it. Adoption without litigation is not judicial validation.

The relevant evidence would be the written legal analyses, the complete policies and enforcement procedures, and controlling decisions in this municipality’s federal circuit. An appellate ruling upholding materially identical language would warrant reassessment. On the information supplied, my appraisal remains: **“unconstructive tone” and “non-civic attitude” grant excessive discretion to suppress disfavored expression and should be replaced with objective, conduct-based disruption criteria.**
